menu "UPS to ESP32 Module board"

choice UPSA_BOARD
    prompt "Target board"
    default UPSA_BOARD_REV_A
    help
        Selects the pin map and peripheral set.

        Rev A is the board that exists and ships. The Freenove devkit target
        that firmware was brought up on before Rev A was made has been
        removed; a future revision is added here as a second option.

        Application code must never reference a GPIO number directly. Use
        the BOARD_* macros from board.h.

config UPSA_BOARD_REV_A
    bool "UPS to ESP32 Module, Rev A PCB"

endchoice

# Which boards can carry a display at all. Not user-visible: it is a fact about
# the board, and UPSA_HAVE_OLED is hidden entirely where it is false. The
# display component stays in the tree for Rev B.
#
# NOT REV A. Its OLED header reads 3V3, GND, SDA, SCL from pin 1; the modules
# people buy read GND, VCC, SCL, SDA, so one seated in it is powered backwards.
# That happened to the first Rev A board on 2026-09-25: the board got hot and
# the module died. Offering a display build for Rev A invites a repeat. A later
# revision whose header matches the modules adds itself here.
config UPSA_BOARD_OLED_OK
    bool
    # Rev B adds: default y if UPSA_BOARD_REV_B
    default y if UPSA_REVA_OLED_VCC_FIRST

# The one way a display works on Rev A, and never in a release: a module whose
# pins read VCC, GND, SCL, SDA. Its power pins then match J3 exactly, and the
# swapped SDA/SCL pair is undone in firmware -- the S3 routes I2C to any pin.
# release.yml fails a build with this set, because a customer with a standard
# GND-first module would plug it in and kill it.
config UPSA_REVA_OLED_VCC_FIRST
    bool "Rev A, bench only: display on a VCC-first module"
    depends on UPSA_BOARD_REV_A
    default n
    help
        ONLY for a module whose pins are labelled VCC, GND, SCL, SDA, in that
        order. Rev A's J3 reads 3V3, GND, SDA, SCL from pin 1: a standard
        GND-first module seated in it is powered backwards and dies, and no
        firmware setting can prevent that. Read the labels on the module in
        hand before plugging it in, with VCC into J3's square pad.

        Swaps SDA and SCL (GPIO9/GPIO8) for the display, and enables it.
        Never set in a release build; CI refuses one that has it.

config UPSA_HAVE_OLED
    bool "Enable optional I2C OLED status display"
    depends on UPSA_BOARD_OLED_OK
    default y if UPSA_REVA_OLED_VCC_FIRST
    default n
    help
        The web interface is the primary UI. The OLED is a debug convenience
        and is the single most expensive item on the BOM, so it is off by
        default, and firmware does not require it.

        Only offered on boards whose header takes a standard module as
        seated. Rev A's does not; see UPSA_BOARD_OLED_OK.

config UPSA_MEM_DIAG
    bool "Log heap and task stack use every 10 s (diagnostics)"
    default n
    select FREERTOS_USE_TRACE_FACILITY
    help
        Logs internal heap free, lowest-ever free and largest free block, and
        every task's unused stack. For sizing stacks and buffers against
        measured use rather than guesses; off in releases, where the trace
        facility it needs costs a little RAM of its own.

config UPSA_VBUS_CYCLE_MS
    int "VBUS off-time when power-cycling a wedged UPS interface (ms)"
    default 2000
    range 250 10000

endmenu
